  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBroad upper level troughing currently remains in place over the \neastern CONUS with an upper low to our south near the Great Lakes, \nand another upper low to our north over east central Quebec. \nThe Great Lakes low will gradually drift east southeast through \nthe day before it becomes absorbed in the parent trough. The \nQuebec low will then dumbbell down into southern Quebec tonight \nand into northern New England on Saturday/Saturday night. This \npattern will lead to slightly below normal high temperatures \nthrough the weekend but lows will be well below normal for most \nareas, mainly in the 20s. This pattern will also lead to \nperiodic snow showers around and north of the mountains on \nSaturday and Sunday. Saturday/Saturday night will likely see the\nbest coverage as that is when the upper low should move \noverhead. Any accumulations should remain light but a few of the\nhigher peaks could see an inch or two, especially across \nnorthern New Hampshire and northwest Maine.\n\nThe upper low and trough will finally start to push east on Sunday \nwhich will lead to diminishing precipitation chances by the \nafternoon and evening. Ridging then starts to nudge to begin the \nwork week, which will lead to a gradual warming trend back to above \nnormal temperatures. On Monday, highs will mainly range from the \nupper 30s to upper 40s, but we will be back to widespread highs in \nthe upper 40s to upper 50s by midweek with a few lower 60s possible \nacross the south. With high pressure dominating, next wee should \nremain dry. The next trough may approach at the end of the week into \nnext weekend but nothing looks impactful at the moment.",
